<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Conrad von Hötzendorf</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf was Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> of the Austro-Hungarian army. He repeatedly urged preventive war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and his aggressive offensive plans in 1914 exceeded the army's capabilities, contributing to heavy early losses for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-conrad-von-hotzendorf.jpg" alt="Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HOTZENDORF,_GEN._CONRAD_VON._GENERAL_STAFF,_GERMANY_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Franz Xaver Josef Conrad von Hötzendorf was born on 11 November 1852 in Penzing, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, into an Austrian officers' family. His father, a retired colonel of hussars, had fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. He was educated at the Theresian Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>, took an interest in natural science and in Darwin's theory of evolution, and was commissioned in 1871. After the Kriegsschule he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> in 1876, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> after the Treaty of Berlin and became a popular professor of tactics in Vienna in 1888. His books on infantry tactics sold well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the suggestion of Archduke Franz Ferdinand he became Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> in November 1906. He saw conflict as always inevitable and held that the fate of nations was decided on the battlefield rather than at diplomatic conferences. He proposed preventive war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1906, in 1908 to 1909, in 1912 to 1913 and in May 1914, and Hew Strachan counts twenty-five such proposals between 1 January 1913 and 1 January 1914. His differences with Foreign Minister Aehrenthal, who rejected his plan for preventive war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, led to his dismissal in 1911, and he returned in December 1912.</p><p>His war plans assumed more soldiers than were available, with better training than they had, and relied on railway timetables of the 1870s. In August 1914 an army of 460,000 men sent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was defeated, and the offensive against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> brought the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a>, though Conrad's forces won at Limanowa in December 1914. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> suffered nearly a million casualties, many in efforts to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Przemyśl</a>, which surrendered in April 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 1915 his troops depended increasingly on German support and command. Emperor Charles I made him a field marshal in November 1916 but dismissed him as Chief of Staff in March 1917, after the failure of his Trentino Offensive. He then commanded an army group on the Italian front until he retired in the summer of 1918. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> von Reininghaus in 1915 after a long affair, and after his death in 1925 a journal was found that held more than 3,000 letters to her which he had never sent. The historian John Leslie describes him as a loner who was politically inept.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chief of Staff <b>Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf</b>'s offensive planning was badly at odds with the army's real capabilities: in 1914 the invasions of Serbia were repulsed at Cer and the Kolubara, while in Galicia the army was routed around Lemberg (Lviv), losing much of its trained peacetime officer…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Penzing, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-mergentheim">Bad Mergentheim</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.3&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Penzing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4929 478 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5379 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5379 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.3&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
